3:One Question Answering Model for Many Languages with Cross-lingual Dense Passage Retrieval
11:Abstract We present CORA , a C ross-lingual O pen- R etrieval A nswer Generation model 1 that can answer questions across many languages even when language-speciﬁc 2 annotated data or knowledge sources are unavailable. We introduce a new dense 3 passage retrieval algorithm that is trained to retrieve documents across languages 4 for a question. Combined with a multilingual autoregressive generation model, 5 CORA answers directly in the target language without any external translation or 6 in-language retrieval modules as used in prior work. To train CORA, we extend 7 available annotated data using an iterative training approach that improves retrieval 8 for languages with limited resources. Our results show that CORA substantially 9 outperforms the previous state of the art on multilingual open question answering 10 benchmarks across 26 languages, 9 of which are unseen during training. Our 11 analyses show the signiﬁcance of cross-lingual retrieval and generation in many 12 languages, particularly under low-resource settings. We will publicly release our 13 code and model. 14
21: followed by a reader to extracts the answer from 19 retrieved passages. However, extending this approach to multilingual open QA poses new challenges. 20 Answering many questions requires retrieving answers in knowledge sources of other languages than 21 the original question since many languages have limited reference documents or the question inquires 22 about concepts from other cultures (Asai et al., 2021; Lin et al., 2020). Yet, large-scale open QA 23 training data are not available across languages. 24 To address these challenges, previous work in multilingual open QA (Ture and Boschee, 2016; Asai 25 et al., 2021) translates questions into English, applies an English open QA system to answer in 26 English, and then translates answers back to the target language. This pipeline approach suffers 27 from error propagation of the machine translation component into the downstream QA, especially for 28 low-resource languages. Moreover, it is not able to answer questions whose answers can be found 29 in resources written in languages other than English or the target languages. For example, when a 30 Japanese question asks about a Korean actor, it is useful to retrieve passages from Korean Wikipedia, 31 not just English and Japanese. In this paper, we introduce a single QA model that answers questions 32 in any language leveraging an iterative training and data expansion approach to enable cross-lingual 33 retrieval and answer generation. 34 Our method (called CORA, Fig. 1) extends the retrieve-then-generate approach of English open QA 35 with a single cross-lingual retriever and a generator that do not rely on language-speciﬁc or machine 36 Submitted to 35th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2021). Do not distribute. Question Encoder Maximum Inner Product Search mDPR : Multilingual Dense Passage Retriever mGEN : Multilingual Answer Generator Generator q L : quién escribió la canción eye of the tiger ( who wrote the song eye of the tiger ) Eye of the Tiger «Eye of the Tiger» fue escrita Frankie Sullivan y Jim Peterik Eye of the Tiger Eye of the Tiger ןומזפ אוה קורה תקהל לש העוציבב ו ובתכש , רבייברוס תינקירמאה ןבילאס יקנרפ Passage Encoder Frankie Sullivan a L translation modules. The multilingual retrieval module produces dense embeddings of a question and 37 all multilingual passages, thereby retrieving passages across languages. The generation component is 38 trained to output an answer in the target language conditioned on the retrieved multilingual passages. 39 Those two components are iteratively trained to address limitations of the current multilingual QA 40 datasets. In particular, each iteration proceeds over two stages by updating model parameters with 41 available training data and expanding the training data cross-lingually by Wikipedia language links 42 and model predictions. This approach does not require any additional human annotations or machine 43 translation, and can be applied to many new languages with low resources. 44 Our experiments show that CORA advances the state of the art on two multilingual open QA datasets, 45 X OR -T Y D I QA (Asai et al., 2021) and MKQA (Longpre et al., 2020), across 26 typologically 46 diverse languages; CORA achieves gains of 21.4 and 4.9 F1 points in X OR -T Y D I QA and MKQA 47 respectively, where MKQA data is not used for training. Moreover, CORA achieves F1 scores of 48 roughly 30 over 9 languages on MKQA that have no training data or even reference Wikipedia 49 documents, outperforming the state-of-the-art approach by 5.4 F1. Our controlled experiments and 50 human analyses illustrate the impact of cross-lingual retrieval in improving QA performance. For 51 example, we observe that CORA can ﬁnd answers to 20% of the multilingual QA questions that are 52 valid but are originally labeled as unanswerable, by using cross-lingual retrieval. 53
29: 61 CORA directly retrieves multilingual passages for questions in any language and generates answers 62 in the target language conditioned on those retrieved passages. More formally, CORA inference 63 consists of two steps of (i) retrieving passages p multi and (ii) generating the answer a L from the 64 retrieved passages. 65 , Multilingual Dense Passage Retriever (mDPR). mDPR extends Dense Passage Retriever (DPR; 66 Karpukhin et al., 2020) to a multilingual setting. mDPR uses an iterative training approach to 67 ﬁne-tune a pre-trained multilingual language model (e.g., mBERT; Devlin et al., 2019) to encode 68 passages and questions separately (§ 2.2). Once training is done, the representations for all passages 69 from C multi are computed ofﬂine and stored in local storage. At inference, mDPR independently 70 obtains d -dimensional ( d = 768 ) encodings of the question e q L = mBERT q ( q L ) and a passage 71 e p L = mBERT p ( p ) , where a passage p is a ﬁxed-length sequence of tokens from multilingual 72 documents. It retrieves k passages with the k highest relevance scores to the question, where the 73 2 Initial QA data mDPR 1 mGEN 1 mGEN 2 mDPR 2 Negative Training Inference mDPR retrieved data WikiData Positive New training data 1. Initial mDPR Training 2. Training mGEN from mDPR data 3. Cross-lingual Data Expansion 4. Automatic Labeling Wikidata-based data 5. Iteratively training mDPR & mGEN mGEN 1 outputs: ✔ ❌ relevance score between a passage p and a question q L is estimated by the inner product of the 74 encoding vectors, ⟨ e q L , e p ⟩ . The retrieved passages p multi can be in any language in C multi . 75 Multilingual Answer Generator (mGEN). We use a multilingual sequence-to-sequence model 76 (e.g., mT5; Xue et al., 2021) to generate answers in the target language token-by-token autoregres- 77 sively given the retrieved multilingual passages p multi . We choose a generation approach because it 78 can generate an answer in the target language L from passages across different languages. 1 Moreover, 79 the generator can be adapted to new and unseen languages, some of which may have little or no 80 translation training data. Speciﬁcally, the generator outputs the sequence probability for a L as follows: 81 where a Li denotes the i -th token in the answer, and T is the length of the answer. We append a 83 language tag to the question to indicate the target language. We can remove the dependency on 84 p multi and skip the retrieval step (i.e., close-book model), but this results in a performance drop (§ 4). 85
33: 86 We introduce an iterative training approach that encourages cross-lingual retrieval and answer 87 generation conditioned on multilingual passages (sketched in Fig. 2 and Alg. 1). Each iteration 88 proceeds over two stages: parameter updates (§ 2.2.1) where mDPR and mGEN are trained 89 given the current training data and cross-lingual data expansion (§ 2.2.2) where training data are 90 automatically expanded by Wikipedia language links and model predictions. Our data expansion 91 addresses the limitation of the current multilingual QA datasets. 92 Initial training data. Training data is initialized by combining multilingual QA (T Y D I QA, Clark 93 et al., 2020 and X OR -T Y D I QA) and English open QA datasets (Natural Questions, Kwiatkowski 94 et al., 2019). One training instance from these datasets comprises a question, a positive passage, and 95 an answer. For each question, we use positive passages for the other questions in the training batch as 96 negative passages (Karpukhin et al., 2020). Note that annotations in the multilingual QA datasets 97 are limited: positive passages are taken either from English (Asai et al., 2021) or the question’s 98 language (Clark et al., 2020). Further, most of the non-English languages are not covered. Indeed, 99 when we only train mDPR on this initial set, it learns to retrieve passages in the same languages or 100 similar languages with irrelevant context. 101
37: 102 mDPR updates. (line 3 in Alg. 1). Let D = {⟨ q Li , p + i , p − i, 1 , ⋯ , p − i,n ⟩} mi = 1 be m training instances. 103 Each instance consists of a question q Li , a passage that answers the question (positive passage) p + i , and 104 n passages that do not answer the question (negative passages) p − i,j . mDPR is updated by minimizing 105 the negative log likelihood of positive passages: 106 (2) 1 An alternative approach of answer extraction requires translation for all language pairs (Asai et al., 2021). 3 Data: Input QA pairs: ( q L ,a L ) 1 initialize training data B 1 = ( q L ,a L ,p gold ) , L = { Eng ,L } ; 2 while t < T do 3 Θ tmDPR ← Train ( θ t − 1 mDPR , B t ) /* Train mDPR */ 4 p multi ← mDPR ( q L , embedding ( C multi )) /* Retrieve passages */ 5 θ tmGEN ← Train ( θ t − 1 mGEN , ( q L ,a L , p multi )) /* Train mGEN */ 6 For L == Eng , p multi + = LangLink ( q L , C multi )) /* Mine data using Wikidata * / 7 For p i ∈ p multi : if mGEN ( q L ,p i ) == a L then positives.add ( p i ) else negatives.add ( p i ) 8 B t + 1 += ( q L ,a L ,positives,negatives ) /* Add new training data */ 9 t ← t + 1 10 end mGEN updates (lines 4-5 in Alg. 1). After updating mDPR, we use mDPR to retrieve top k passages 107 p multi for each q L . Given these pairs of the question and the retrieved passages ( q L , p multi ) as input, 108 mGEN is trained to generate answer a L autoregressively (Eq. (1)) and minimize the cross-entropy 109 loss. To train the model to generate in languages not covered by the original datasets, we translate a L 110 to other languages using Wikipedia language links and create new synthetic mGEN training data. 2 111
45: 134 We evaluate CORA on two multilingual open QA datasets across 28 typologically diverse languages. 3 135 CORA achieves state-of-the-art performance across 26 languages, and greatly outperforms previous 136 approaches that use language-speciﬁc models such as question translation. 137 2 This data augmentation is only done after the third epoch of initial training to prevent the model from overﬁtting to synthetic data . See the appendix for more detail. 3 A full list of the language family and script types are in the appendix . 4
49: 138 Multilingual open QA datasets differ in covered languages, annotation schemes, and target application 139 scenarios. We evaluate F1 and EM scores over the questions with answer annotations from two 140 datasets, following the common evaluation practice in open QA (Lee et al., 2019). 141 X OR -T Y D I QA. X OR -T Y D I QA (Asai et al., 2021) is a multilingual open QA dataset consisting 142 of 7 languages, where questions are originally from T Y D I QA (Clark et al., 2020) and posed by 143 information-seeking native speakers. The answers are annotated by extracting spans from Wikipedia 144 in the same language of the question ( in-language data ) or by translating English spans extracted 145 from English Wikipedia to the target language ( cross-lingual data ). 146 MKQA. MKQA (Longpre et al., 2020) is an evaluation dataset created by translating 10k Natural 147 Questions (Kwiatkowski et al., 2019) to 25 target languages. The parallel data enables us to compare 148 the models’ performance across typologically diverse languages. This dataset does not have any 149 training data, and only ﬁve languages are covered in the X OR -T Y D I QA training data. 150 Collection of multilingual documents. We use the Wikipedia dumps of 13 diverse languages from 151 all X OR -T Y D I QA languages and a subset of MKQA languages, leading to about 31.5M passages. 4 152 Several MKQA languages are not included so that we can evaluate over unseen languages and make 153 indexing and inference computationally feasible. We chose the 13 languages to cover languages with 154 a large number of Wikipedia articles and a variety of both Latin and non-Latin scripts. 155 Training-evaluation language splits. CORA is trained on X OR -T Y D I QA; mDPR uses gold 156 passages from languages in X OR -T Y D I QA and also synthetically mined data from 13 languages (as 157 explained in § 2.2.2). The mGEN model is trained on X OR -T Y D I QA and synthetically generated 158 data for 17 languages (§ 2.2.1). We call languages on which we train CORA with annotated data or 159 synthetically generated data seen languages, while call others unseen languages. MKQA is only used 160 for evaluation, and 9 of its languages are never included during training. 161
53: 162 We compare CORA with the following strong baselines adopted from Asai et al. (2021). 163 Translate-test (MT + DPR). As used in most previous work (e.g., Asai et al., 2021), this method 164 translates the question to English, extracts an answer in English using DPR, and then translates the 165 answer back to the target language. The translation models are obtained from MarianMT (Junczys- 166 Dowmunt et al., 2018) and trained on the OPUS-MT dataset (Tiedemann, 2012). 167 Monolingual baseline (BM 25). This baseline retrieves passages in the target language and extracts 168 the answer from the retrieved passages. Due to the lack of training data in most of the target languages, 169 we use a BM25-based lexical Pyserini implementation (Lin et al., 2021). We then feed the retrieved 170 documents to a multilingual QA model to extract ﬁnal answers. 171 MT+Mono. This baseline combines results from the translate-test method (MT+DPR) and the 172 monolingual method (BM25) to enable retrieving passages in both English and the target language. 173 Following Asai et al. (2021), we prioritize predictions from the monolingual pipeline if they are over 174 a certain threshold; otherwise we output predictions from the translate-test method. 5 175 Closed-book baseline. This model uses an mT5-base sequence-to-sequence model that takes a 176 question as input and generates answers in the target language without any retrieval component at 177 inference time (Roberts et al., 2020). This baseline assesses the models’ ability of memorizing and 178 retrieving knowledge from its parameters without using the knowledge from reference documents. 179 CORA details. For all experiments, we use a single retriever (mDPR) and a generator (mGEN) that 180 use the same passage embeddings. mDPR uses multilingual-BERT-base-uncased, 6 and the generator 181 uses mT5-base for ﬁne-tuning. We ﬁrst ﬁne-tune mDPR using gold passages from Natural Question, 182 and then further ﬁne-tune it using X OR -T Y D I QA and T Y D I QA’s gold passage data. We exclude 183 4 Downloaded from https://archive.org/details/wikimediadownloads?and%5B%5D= year%3A%222019%22 . 5 For the languages not supported by Pyserini, we always output translate-test’ predictions. 6 XLM-RoBERTa (Conneau et al., 2020) did not improve our results. 5 the training questions in Natural Questions and T Y D I QA used to create the MKQA or X OR -T Y D I 184 QA evaluation set. We run two iterations of CORA training. All hyperparameters are in the appendix. 185
61: 187 X OR -T Y D I QA. Table 1 reports the scores of CORA and the baselines in X OR -T Y D I QA. CORA, 188 which only uses a single retriever and a single generator, outperforms the baselines and the previous 189 state-of-the-art model on X OR -T Y D I QA by a large margin across all 7 languages. CORA achieves 190 a gain of 21.4 macro-averaged F1 points over the previous state-of-the-art method, which uses the 191 black-box APIs of Google Translate and Google Custom Search. 192 MKQA. Tables 2 and 3 report the F1 scores of CORA and the baselines on MKQA under seen and 193 unseen settings. 7 Longpre et al. (2020) only report results in a simpliﬁed setting with gold passages 194 given in advance, where retrieval is unnecessary. CORA signiﬁcantly outperforms the baselines in all 195 languages by a large margin except for Arabic and English. CORA yields larger improvements from 196 the translate-test baseline in the languages that are distant from English and with limited training 197 data such as Malay (MS; 29.2 vs. 12.6) and Hebrew (He; 15.3 vs. 8.9). The performance drop 198 of the translate-test model from English to other languages indicates the error propagation from 199 the translation process. BM25 performs very poorly in some low-resource languages such as Thai 200 because of the lack of answer content in the target languages’ Wikipedia. MT+Mono underpeforms 201 the MT+DPR baseline in MKQA since it is challenging to rerank answers from two separate methods 202 with uncaliberated conﬁdence scores. In contrast, CORA uses a single retrieval model to retrieve 203 passages across languages, achieving around 30 F1 in a majority of the 26 languages. 204 Ablations: Impact of CORA components. We compare CORA with the following three variants 205 to study the impact of different components on X OR -T Y D I QA and a subset of MKQA ( ∼ 5% of 206 the evaluation set). (i) mDPR 1 + mGEN 1 only trains CORA using labeled annotated data and 207 7 Following previous work in open QA, but different from the ofﬁcial script of MKQA (Longpre et al., 2020), we disregard the questions labeled as “no answer” to ensure that a model cannot increase aggregate F1 and EM scores simply by predicting “no answer.” As shown in our human analysis, it is difﬁcult to prove an answer does not exist in the millions of multilingual documents even if the annotation says so. 6 measures the impact of the iterative training. (ii) DPR (trained NQ) + mGEN replaces mDPR 208 with a multilingual BERT-based DPR trained on English data from Natural Questions (NQ) and 209 encodes all passages in C multi . This conﬁguration assesses the impact of cross-lingual training data. 210 (iii) CORA, C multi ={Eng.} only retrieves from English during inference. This variant evaluates if 211 English reference documents sufﬁce to answer multilingual questions. 212 Results in Table 4 show performance drops in all three variants. This leads to the following conclu- 213 sions: (i) iterative learning and data mining process is useful, (ii) mDPR trained with cross-lingual 214 data outperforms DPR with multi-lingual BERT, and (iii) languages other than English are important 215 in answering multilingual questions. 216
65: 54 DPR NQ) 52 46 12 Retrieval performance and relationship to the ﬁnal QA performance. We evaluate CORA’s 218 retrieval performance on MKQA using two recall metrics that measure the percentage of questions 219 with at least one passage among the top 10 that includes a string in an answer set in the target language 220 ( R L @10 ) or in the union of answer sets from all languages that is available in MKQA ( R multi @10 ). 221 Table 5 reports retrieval results for mDPR and multilingual-bert based DPR trained on NQ ( DPR 222 (NQ) , which is equivalent to (ii) from ablations). We observe that mDPR performs well in Indo- 223 European languages with Latin script, even when the language is unseen. Interestingly, there is a 224 signiﬁcant performance gap between R L @10 and R multi @10 in languages with non-Latin script (e.g., 225 Japanese, Russian, Chinese), which can indicate that our model often retrieves relevant documents 226 from other languages with Latin scripts such as English or Spanish in those languages as well. Our 227 mDPR outperforms DPR (NQ) by a large margin in unseen languages with limited resources, as 228 7 Ja Es retrieval errors 28 48 different lang 18 0 incorrect answer 22 36 annotation error 22 12 invalid question 10 4 shown in Table 3. Nevertheless, we still see low performance in Khmer or Thai even with R multi @10 229 metric. We also observe that passage and query embeddings for those languages are far from other 230 languages, which can be further studied in a future work. We provide two-dimensional encoded 231 document representations in Appendix. 232 Visualizing cross-lingual retrieval. Fig. 3 breaks down retrieved reference languages for each 233 target language. Our multilingual retrieval model often retrieves documents from the target language 234 (if its reference documents are available), English, or its typologically similar languages. For example, 235 CORA often retrieves Spanish passages for Portuguese questions and Japanese passages for Chinese 236 questions; Japanese and Chinese overlap in script. 237 To further evaluate this, we conduct a controlled experiment: we remove Spanish, Swedish and 238 Indonesian documents’ embeddings and evaluate CORA on relevant languages Danish, Portuguese 239 and Malay. We observe performance drops of 1.0 F1 in Danish, 0.6 F1 in Portuguese, and 3.4 F1 240 in Malay. This illustrates that cross-lingual retrieval from closer languages in fact helps CORA to 241 achieve better performance in those languages. 242 Error analysis and qualitative examples. Table 6 analyzes errors made by CORA by manually 243 inspecting 50 Japanese and Spanish wrong predictions from MKQA. We observe six error categories: 244 (a) retrieval error, (b) generation language error (different lang), (c) incorrect answer generation 245 (incorrect answer), (d) answer annotation errors (e.g., a correct alias isn’t covered by gold answers, or 246 Wikipedia information is inconsistent with English.), and (e) ambiguous or ill-formed questions such 247 as “who won X this year” (invalid question). The table shows that in both Japanese and Spanish, the 248 retrieval errors are dominant. In Japanese, CORA often generates the correct answers in English, not 249 in Japanese, which hurts its ﬁnal performance. 250 Fig. 4 shows some qualitative examples. The ﬁrst example shows an error in (b): mGEN is generating 251 an answer in Russian, not in French though the answer itself is correct. This type of errors happen 252 especially when retrieved passages are in other languages than the target and English. 253 8 Human evaluation on cross-lingual retrieval results. To observe how cross-lingual retrieval 254 between distant languages is actually helping, we sample 25 Norwegian questions for which Spanish 255 passages are included among the top 10 retrieved results. As seen in Fig. 3, CORA retrieves Spanish 256 passages for 6.8% of the Norwegian questions. A bilingual Spanish native speaker judges if the 257 retrieved Spanish paragraphs actually answer the given Norwegian questions. We found that in 96% 258 of the cases, the retrieved Spanish paragraphs are relevant in answering the question. One such 259 example is presented in Fig. 4, second example. 260 Human analysis on unanswerable questions. CORA retrieves passages from a larger multilingual 261 document collection, which may further improve the answer coverage. MKQA includes the questions 262 that are marked as unanswerable by English native speakers given English knowledge sources. We 263 sample 400 unanswerable Japanese questions whose top one retrieved passage is from non-English 264 Wikipedia articles. Among these 329 questions are ill formed (also discussed in Asai and Choi, 2021). 265 For 17 out of the 71 remaining questions, CORA’s answer is correct. This ﬁnding indicates the 266 signiﬁcance of cross-lingual retrieval and potential room for improvement in annotating multilingual 267 open QA datasets. The third example in Fig. 4 shows one of these examples. 268
69: English and non-English open QA. Despite the rapid progress in open QA (Chen et al., 2017; 270 Karpukhin et al., 2020; Lewis et al., 2020; Izacard and Grave, 2021), most prior work has been 271 exclusively on English. Several prior attempts to build multilingual open QA often rely on additional 272 MT or language-speciﬁc retrieval models (Asai et al., 2021; Ture and Boschee, 2016). 273 Many-languages-one models. Recently, several work introduced single multilingual models for 274 many languages by using pre-trained multilingual models such as mBERT or mT5 in several NLP 275 tasks (e.g., entity linking: Botha et al., 2020; De Cao et al., 2021; semantic role labeling: Mulcaire 276 et al., 2019; Lyu et al., 2019; Fei et al., 2020). This work conducts the ﬁrst large-scale study 277 of a uniﬁed multilingual open QA model across many languages, and we show state-of-the-art 278 performance in 26 typologically diverse languages. 279 Synthetic data creation for multilingual machine reading comprehension. Several recent work 280 augment multilingual machine reading comprehension training data by generating new question 281 and answer pairs from randomly sampled non-English Wikipedia paragraphs using a sequence-to- 282 sequence model (Riabi et al., 2020; Shakeri et al., 2020). This work focuses on multilingual open QA, 283 which involve cross-lingual retrieval going beyond extracting answers from multilingual passages. 284 To this end, we augment existing training data by using trained models and language links, which can 285 be further improved by applying similar question generation methods. 286 Societal impacts. We will make our code publicly available. CORA can perform open QA in unseen 287 languages and could beneﬁt the society in building QA systems for low-resource languages such as 288 African languages, hence enabling research in that direction. By removing the necessity of using 289 external blackbox APIs, we can examine and address wrong answers because of its prediction error 290 or misinformation that exists in Wikipedia. This would help us to mitigate the potential negative 291 impact by CORA outputting a wrong answer when it used by people who seek information. 292
73: 293 To address the information needs from many non-English speakers, a QA system has to conduct 294 cross-lingual passage retrieval. This work presents CORA, a uniﬁed multilingual open QA model 295 that retrieves multilingual passages and generates answers in the target languages, conditioned on 296 the retrieved passages. CORA does not require language-speciﬁc translation or retrieval components 297 and can even answer questions in unseen new languages. We conduct extensive experiments on two 298 multilingual open QA datasets across 28 languages, 26 of which CORA advances the state of the 299 art, outperforming competitive models by up to 20 F1 points. Our extensive analysis and manual 300 evaluation reveal that CORA effectively retrieves semantically relevant passages beyond language 301 boundaries, and can even ﬁnd answers to the questions that are previously considered unanswerable 302 due to lack of sufﬁcient evidence in annotation languages. 303 9
